  "title": "The Mystery of the Missing Wheels",
  "hook": "Every spare wheel has vanished the night before Junior Build Day — is there a wheel thief in the garage?",
  "reading_minutes": 7,
  "core_lesson": "Know what you have before you build: count it, sort it, label it.",
  "new_terms": [
    "inventory",
    "spare"
  ],
  "adult_safety_note": "Children only handle cardboard, paper straws and plastic toy wheels. Auntie Ada (an adult) uses the scissors and pokes the axle holes.",
  "localisation_key_prefix": "s01e02",
  "editorial_status": "draft",
  "learning_objectives": [
    {
      "topic": "Number",
      "goal": "Four groups of four make 16 (4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 16, 4 × 4 = 16)."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Number",
      "goal": "Take away to find what is left: 16 − 12 = 4 spare wheels."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Engineering",
      "goal": "An inventory is a count of the parts you have; check it before you build."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Organising",
      "goal": "Sorting and picture labels make parts easy to find and count again."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Leadership",
      "goal": "Look for clues before blaming anyone; give each person a job that fits them."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Safety",
      "goal": "A grown-up handles scissors and poking holes; children assemble the cardboard cars."
    }
  ],
  "talk_together": [
    "Brian thought there was a wheel thief. What did the clues really show?",
    "What could you sort into groups at home — socks, spoons, toy cars? How many groups?",
    "Why do picture labels help Marcus, who can't read yet?"
  ],
  "complete": {
    "recall": "Before you build: count it, sort it, label it!",
    "question": "Help a grown-up make an inventory of one drawer or toy box. How many of each thing did you find?"
